<article class="env-theorem" id="9438D3">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/9438D3">Theorem <span data-tag="9438D3">3.1.5.1</span>.</a> There exists a sequence of Quillen adjunctions </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \calC _0 \stackrel{F_0}{\rightarrow } \calC _1 \stackrel{F_1}{\rightarrow } \calC _2 \stackrel{F_2}{\rightarrow } \calC _3 \stackrel{F_3}{\rightarrow } \calC _4 \]
</div>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \calC _0 \stackrel{G_0}{\leftarrow } \calC _1 \stackrel{G_1}{\leftarrow } \calC _2 \stackrel{G_2}{\leftarrow } \calC _3 \stackrel{G_3}{\leftarrow } \calC _4 \]
</div>
  <p> which may be described as follows: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(A0)$"><p>
The functor $G_0$ is the forgetful functor from $(\mSet )_{/S}$ to $(\sSet )_{/S}$, which ignores the collection of marked edges. The functor $F_0$ is the left adjoint to $G_0$, which is given by $X \mapsto X^{\flat }$. The Quillen adjunction $(F_0,G_0)$ is a Quillen equivalence if $S$ is a Kan complex. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(A1)$"><p>
The functors $F_1$ and $G_1$ are the identity functors on $(\mSet )_{/S}$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(A2)$"><p>
The functor $F_2$ is the forgetful functor from $(\mSet )_{/S}$ to $(\sSet )_{/S}$, which ignores the collection of marked edges. The functor $G_2$ is the right adjoint to $F_2$, which is given by $X \mapsto X^{\sharp }$. The Quillen adjunction $(F_2,G_2)$ is a Quillen equivalence for <em>every</em> simplicial set $S$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(A3)$"><p>
The functors $F_3$ and $G_3$ are the identity functors on $(\mSet )_{/S}$. The Quillen adjunction $(F_3, G_3)$ is a Quillen equivalence whenever $S$ is a Kan complex. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>